From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] input: rsinput: survive an unbind with the MCU streaming

Unbinding and rebinding serial1-0 while the pad is reporting oopses
the kernel:

  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at 0000000000000028
  pc : input_event+0xc/0x70
  lr : rsinput_rx+0x3bc/0x858
  x0 : 0000000000000000
  Workqueue: ttyHS-qcom_geni_uart flush_to_ldisc

with "input: AYN Odin2 Gamepad as .../input/input37" interleaved in the
trace: the receive path was handling MCU data while probe was still
registering the input device. Afterwards the handshake times out three
times and the UART reports "failed to push data (0 < 23)" until a
reboot.

Two windows let it happen, and both are needed to close it.

rsinput_remove() closes the port but leaves drvdata pointing at memory
devm is about to free. serdev keeps a bound device's ops across an
unbind - they are a static struct - so the next serdev_device_open()
hands the stream straight back to the old callback. rsinput_rx()'s
"if (!drv)" cannot catch that: the pointer is stale, not NULL. Clear
drvdata once the port is closed.

The other window is in probe. drvdata is set before the input device is
allocated, so between those two points drv is valid and drv->input is
NULL - which is exactly the NULL that reached input_event(). Widen the
guard to cover it.

Found by using unbind and rebind to re-send the MCU parameters command,
which only goes out from probe, while sweeping the frame_rate parameter
1019 added. That is a debugging technique rather than something a user
does, but the same shape reaches the suspend and resume path, where the
MCU is quiesced and re-initialised with the port already open.

--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
@@ -499,7 +499,11 @@ static void rsinput_process_data(struct rsinput_driver *drv, const uint8_t *data
 static size_t rsinput_rx(struct serdev_device *serdev, const u8 *data, size_t count) {
     struct rsinput_driver *drv = serdev_device_get_drvdata(serdev);
 
-    if (!drv || !data || count == 0) {
+    /*
+     * drv->input is NULL between probe setting drvdata and registering the
+     * device, and the MCU is already streaming by then on a rebind.
+     */
+    if (!drv || !drv->input || !data || count == 0) {
         dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "Invalid RX data\n");
         goto error;
     }
@@ -608,7 +613,16 @@ static void rsinput_remove(struct serdev_device *serdev) {
     struct rsinput_driver *drv = serdev_device_get_drvdata(serdev);
 
     serdev_device_close(serdev);
+    /*
+     * The receive callback finds the driver through drvdata on every byte,
+     * and serdev keeps this device's ops across an unbind. Leaving drvdata
+     * pointing at what devm is about to free means the next open hands the
+     * MCU's stream to a freed structure, which is not a NULL the callback
+     * can test for. Clear it now the port is shut, while the local drv is
+     * still good for the teardown below.
+     */
+    serdev_device_set_drvdata(serdev, NULL);
     input_unregister_device(drv->input);
     if (drv->enable_gpio)
         gpiod_set_value_cansleep(drv->enable_gpio, 0);
